course: dictionary
lesson: 11346
title: Reading a made-up word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «zib» is not an ordinary English word, so you will not find it in a normal English dictionary. That makes it a good test of reading. You cannot rely on memory, so you have to use the sounds and the words around it.
explain: «Made-up «alien words» like «zib» are read on purpose: they show a child is really reading the sounds and not guessing a word they remember.» So when you meet a made-up word in a story, ask: is it a name, a sound, or a word someone invented?
explain: The context gives clues. If «zib» is a robot's name, a sound effect, or a typing slip for «zip», the sentence will show which. Swap your idea into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Be careful, though. In some languages «zib» is rude slang, so do not use it as a name for a person or thing in your own writing.
fact: «zib» is not an ordinary English word and is not in normal English dictionaries.
fact: It may be a piece of a longer word, a sound or name made up for a story or game, or a word borrowed from another language.
fact: In a story, «zib» could be a made-up name for a creature or robot, a sound effect, or a typing mistake for «zip».
fact: In some languages «zib» is rude slang, so it is best not to use it as a name.
fact: Reading made-up words such as «zib» proves you are decoding the sounds, not guessing from memory.
exercise: In a story, «The robot said zib and its lights flashed.» What is zib most likely to be? | *A made-up name or sound effect for the robot | A kind of sunny day | A type of cake | A large English city
exercise: Which fact about zib is true? | It is the longest word in English | It always means zip | *It is not an ordinary English word, so it is not in normal dictionaries | It is in every English dictionary
exercise: What is the best check for an unknown word like zib? | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Look for a picture of it | Ask a friend to spell it for you
test: You meet «zib» in a book and it is not in your dictionary. What should you do? | Guess a rude meaning | *Use the words and sentence around it to work out what it might be | Stop reading the whole book | Skip the whole chapter forever
test: Why is it best not to use «zib» as a name for something? | It is the name of a country | *In some languages it is rude slang | It is too short to say | It has too many letters
exam: Reading made-up words like «zib» shows what? | That you have never read a book | That the word is in every dictionary | *That you are decoding the sounds, not guessing from memory | That the story is finished
